#!/bin/sh
# The stand-in `notify-send` the headless smoke puts in front of the real one.
#
# The GUI's desktop notification is a PROGRAM it runs, so the honest way to read
# what it sent is to BE that program: this records the real argv the real binary
# built, with no seam in the product and no injection point a shipped path could
# route around.
#
# One line per invocation: the CALLER's pid first, then the argv, with a unit
# separator between fields so an argument containing a space cannot be read as
# two. It exits 0, because the product must not be tested against a notifier
# that is failing.
#
# ## Why the pid
#
# It is what makes a reading an ATTRIBUTION. Both children inherit this PATH, so
# a recorder that logged only the argv would answer *somebody ran the notifier*
# -- and the claim under test is that the CLIENT does, which a daemon doing it
# would satisfy while being the wrong design. `$PPID` is the process that
# spawned this script, which is the client itself: its notifier thread is a
# thread of that process, not a helper of its own.
#
# ## Why this file is TRACKED rather than written by the smoke
#
# Register item 467. A file cannot be executed while any process holds it open
# for writing (`ETXTBSY`), and the smoke both writes this and has the CLIENT
# exec it while the smoke's own threads are forking. Item 465 measured the shape
# on `sprag-gate` at 10 failures in 30 runs. A file nobody writes cannot be busy.
#
# The record lands beside the smoke's state directory rather than at a path
# substituted into these bytes: `$0` is the path the kernel was handed, which is
# this file's link inside `<state>/bin/`.
record="$(dirname "$0")/../notify-send.log"
printf '%s\037' "$PPID" "$@" >> "$record"
printf '\n' >> "$record"
exit 0
